  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Employment for young men and women promoted through entrepreneurship, cooperatives and social enterprises. IGAs and BDS development programmes</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The ongoing conflict has posed numerous challenges for both the refugees and the host communities in Sudan. The conflict in the country has been a major catalyst for the displacement of countless individuals, forcing them to seek refuge in relatively safer states, putting strain on already limited resources, infrastructure and social services. 
Despite the difficult operational environment, the UN team is staying to provide protection and assistance to people affected by the conflict through a three-pronged strategy: 
1. Maintaining critical and lifesaving services in conflict-affected &#x2013; hotspot &#x2013; areas. 
2. Providing urgent assistance to newly displaced and host communities, including displaced refugee populations. 
3. Ensuring basic service delivery continues throughout the country, building on system strengthening in safer states. 
While UN agencies are using humanitarian funding in the hotspot states, as well for the humanitarian response to displaced populations, development funding for basic social and protection services &#x2013; with funding from PROSPECTS II, as well as other key development donors &#x2013; will continue in the safer states. The ILO mission that took place in October 2024 helped to respond to the needs of the constituents that are related to building the capacities of the institutions and identifying and promoting opportunities for livelihood opportunities for the displaced people to engage in income generating activities. The development approach, as in PROSPECTS, is critical to ensure longer-term sustainable solutions to the displacement crisis, which is hindering the humanitarian-development nexus. This is also an effort to maintain gains made before the conflict erupted in strengthening communities to transform camps into settlements. 
The key elements on enterprise development include: developing and promoting productive sectors (agriculture, livestock, industry); focusing on creating several projects with comparative advantage and value-added</narrative>
  </description>
